  "prompt_text": "This figure is a regional map of High Mountain Asia and the Tibetan Plateau showing how glaciers, rivers, lakes, and climate systems are linked. The central mountain belt is marked with glacierized terrain, while large surrounding drainage areas are shaded to distinguish endorheic basins, where water stays inland, from exorheic basins, where rivers drain outward. Major rivers such as the Indus, Amu Darya, Tarim, Yarkant, Yellow, Salween, Brahmaputra, and Qiantang are labeled, along with numerous glacier mass-balance sites and meteorological stations spread across the plateau.\n\nThe most important message is the contrast between moisture sources and drainage patterns across the region. Large arrows show the westerlies entering from the west, the Indian monsoon pushing northward from the south, and the East Asian monsoon reaching in from the east. These atmospheric systems converge over different parts of the plateau and appear to help explain the spatial distribution of glaciers, lakes, and river basins. Blue lakes are concentrated within the interior endorheic zone, while the outer margins feed major river systems flowing toward surrounding lowlands. Overall, the figure summarizes the hydrologic and climatic setting of Asia’s high mountains as a key water source region.",
